    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2BWDp9cgemxzr2hZ9jvXJcG)_\n\n## Merge order\n\n- Base: `main`; no dependency on other open PRs.\n- Touches `sp-citations` only (`.el`, its section in `sp-academic.org`, a new test file).\n- Branch is `claude/project-thread-d7whd6`, not number-prefixed: a cloud session cannot rename a branch after the PR number is known.\n\n## Answer\n\n- **Yes**, the `.bib` files can be read after startup, in idle time, so the first `citar` call finds them parsed.\n- **Yes**, a cache on disk avoids re-reading unchanged files across Emacs sessions.\n- **No git needed**: `citar` already compares size, modification time and a hash of the contents, and only parses a file whose content changed. Asking git would only repeat that (and spawn a process).\n\n## How citar caches (read from `citar-cache.el`)\n\n- Parsed files live in `citar-cache--bibliographies` (memory only, hence the slow first call of every session).\n- Every call checks each file: same size and mtime, nothing is read; changed mtime, the file is hashed and parsed only if the hash differs.\n- A bibliography tied to no buffer is dropped the next time citar releases unused ones, so the preload ties each file to `global`.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Idle preload in `sp-citations` (loaded through `sp-late-modules`): an idle timer loads `citar` (retries if Elpaca has not installed it yet) and reads one file per idle slice (0.3 s idle between files).\n- `C-g` while a file is parsed stops the preload; citar reads the rest when called.\n- Refresh: saving a `.bib` buffer schedules a new pass (covers `sp-fetch-inbox`, which saves the buffer); `sp-bibtex-register` does the same. A pass only checks files that did not change.\n- Disk cache `citar-bibliography.eld` in `sp-cache-dir`: entries plus the size/hash/fields plist, written only after a pass that parsed something. On the next start citar still validates it, so a changed file or changed template fields is parsed again; candidates are formatted again from the entries. A damaged or missing file is ignored.\n- Options: `sp-citations-preload` (t), `sp-citations-preload-delay` (3 s), `sp-citations-preload-refresh-on-save` (t), `sp-citations-preload-persist` (t), `sp-citations-preload-cache-file`.\n- I did not use `sp-async-work` (#468): its slices are timer based, not idle based, and a \"wait until the user is idle\" step would spin. Plain idle timers fit better; the cost is no mode-line progress.\n- Docs: new section in `sp-academic.org` as bullets; no CHANGELOG edit; no `when-let`.\n\n## Tested\n\n- `tests/sp-citations-preload-test.el`, 6 tests on Emacs 29.3 with a stand-in for `citar-cache` (all pass): all files read and tied to `global`; second pass reads nothing; new session uses the disk cache without parsing; a changed file is parsed again; damaged cache ignored; an error stops quietly.\n- `tools/check-tangle.sh`: the `.el` is what the doc tangles to.\n\n## Not tested\n\n- Nothing ran against the real `citar`, `parsebib` and your `~/Documents/bibliography`: the container has no citar. The internal functions (`citar-cache--get-bibliography`, `--make-bibliography`, struct accessors) were read from citar's `main` branch, so check them against your installed version.\n- No timings were measured: how long the preload takes, and how long the first `citar` call takes with and without the disk cache, are unknown. The log line `Updating bibliography ...done (N seconds)` will show per-file parse times.\n- Whether a parse of a large file feels like a freeze when you start typing (a file cannot be split).\n- Cache read time on disk for your whole library.\n- External changes (`git pull` in `bibliography/`) are found at the next `citar` call, not in the background.\n\n## Manual testing\n\n- [x] `emacs -Q --batch -l tests/sp-citations-preload-test.el -f ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit` passes\n- [ ] Restart Emacs, wait about 5 s idle: `*Messages*` shows `Updating bibliography ...` for each file, then nothing more\n- [ ] `M-x citar-open` right after: opens with no `Updating bibliography` message and no wait\n- [ ] Quit Emacs, check `citar-bibliography.eld` exists in your cache dir; restart: the idle pass should not parse (no `Updating` lines)\n- [ ] Edit and save one `.bib` file: after the idle delay only that file is read again; `citar-open` shows the change\n- [ ] File an article with `sp-fetch-inbox`: the new entry is found by `citar-open` without delay\n- [ ] `git pull` or edit a `.bib` outside Emacs: `citar-open` still shows the new entries (parses just that file)\n- [ ] Press `C-g` during a long file parse: preload stops, `citar-open` still works\n- [ ] `(setopt sp-citations-preload nil)` and restart: no preload\n- [ ] `M-x sp-citations-fit-layout`: candidates still look right\n\n<details><summary>Revert after testing</summary>\n\n- `git -C ~/dotfiles restore .` for local edits, then `git -C ~/dotfiles switch main && git -C ~/dotfiles pull`; restart Emacs.\n- Delete `citar-bibliography.eld` in your cache dir if you want a cold start again.\n\n</details>\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWLNiCyfpEM7dFcJmFXN4v)_",
